---
import AfternoonLife from "../../components/graph/AfternoonLife.astro";
import PaperTrip from "../../components/graph/PaperTrip.astro";
import RequestLoop from "../../components/graph/RequestLoop.astro";
import { NOTEBOOK } from "../../i18n/ui";
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
---

<Base
  lang="en"
  page="flows"
  eyebrow="Requests, state and model calls"
  title="Execution and data flows"
  description="How devices communicate, how the hub runs an activity, and how returned sheets inform the next step."
>
  <p class="lede">The hub coordinates devices and activity timing. The cloud API handles model calls. Returned sheets and photographs of visible work provide input for the next step.</p>

  <h2>Who asks whom</h2>

  <p>
    Devices sit around the home and communicate with the hub over the local network. Battery-powered displays use Wi-Fi; the hub can stay out of sight. Arrows represent data exchanges, not cables. The hub initiates requests to the cloud and receives parent decisions in subsequent responses.
  </p>

  <RequestLoop
    title="Who asks whom"
    desc="The hub connects local devices to the cloud API. The API calls models on Microsoft Foundry through the model router and screens generated text with Content Safety. The hub retrieves parent decisions in response to its requests."
    caption="The hub initiates cloud requests and receives decisions in the response. The API calls Foundry through the model router and separately calls Content Safety to screen generated text. Foundry does not call the safety service."
    house="The house"
    cloud="Cloud services"
    parent={{ title: "Family browsers", sub: ["parent decisions · portal photographs"] }}
    writes="HTTPS"
    displays={{ title: "e-paper displays", sub: ["battery · Wi-Fi"] }}
    button={{ title: "a button", sub: ["answered in the reply"] }}
    hub={{
      title: "Hub",
      sub: ["mini-PC on the home network", "out of sight · fixed power supply"],
    }}
    paper={{ title: "printer · scanner · camera", sub: ["pages and photographs"] }}
    shows="via Wi-Fi"
    press="a press"
    sheet="via local network"
    api={{ title: "cloud API", sub: ["pending decisions and photographs"] }}
    router={{ title: "model router", sub: ["the only module allowed a model SDK"] }}
    gate={{ title: "Content Safety", sub: ["checks generated text"] }}
    foundry={{ title: "Microsoft Foundry", sub: ["language · vision · image"] }}
    asks={["scheduled", "HTTPS request"]}
    answers={["decisions in", "the response"]}
  />

  <p class="note">
    The models run on Microsoft Foundry. Reading a sheet therefore requires cloud connectivity.
  </p>

  <h2>The life of an activity</h2>

  <p>An activity is a graph of steps called moments, with duration options, help and paths to the ending. The hub follows its transitions as time and returns change. A planned <code>ask</code> requests a continuation from the cloud API using the reading of returned material; other transitions continue through the existing plan.</p>

  <AfternoonLife
    title="The life of an activity"
    desc="The hub requests an activity. The cloud API generates, validates and screens it, then waits for parent approval. The hub retrieves approved activities. A returned reading selects a moment or requests a checked continuation without another parent approval."
    caption="The left return edge covers permitted format and structural repairs only. A Content Safety refusal blocks the result. The right return edge is a planned continuation based on returned material. The hub's timer can also start the ending without receiving a return; that time-driven path is not drawn here."
    idea={{ title: "The hub requests an activity", sub: ["preferences · optional brief"] }}
    devise={{
      title: "A model writes the whole activity",
      sub: ["moments, help and ending paths"],
    }}
    checks={{
      title: "Format, checks and safety",
      sub: ["blocking checks before storage"],
    }}
    waits={{ title: "The API awaits approval", sub: ["the parent reads the activity"] }}
    begins={{ title: "The hub starts the activity", sub: ["if enough scheduled time remains"] }}
    moment={{ title: "The hub executes a moment", sub: ["instructions · pages · responses"] }}
    cameBack={{ title: "What came back", sub: ["marks, or blank"] }}
    ends={{ title: "The activity concludes", sub: ["with its planned ending"] }}
    ask={{
      title: "ask",
      sub: [
        "the hub sends the material reading",
        "the API checks the continuation",
        "without a second parent approval",
      ],
    }}
    refused="repair"
    passes="passes"
    approving="approved; next hub poll"
    nextMoment="the next moment"
    askEdge="ask"
    continuation="a continuation, in the same format"
    closes="it closes"
  />

  <p>
    Generation permits one format repair and a separate repair after validation; continuation has no repair. The hub respects the schedule and daily activity limit. It starts the ending path at its first timer check within thirty minutes of the scheduled end. Consecutive moments that collect no response advance without waiting for their declared durations.
  </p>

  <h2>The paper round trip</h2>

  <p>The parent assigns the return function to cameras and scanners. At each collection moment the hub chooses one compatible assigned device, names it on the display and keeps that choice until the return ends. Paper can use either kind; constructions and objects require a camera. A scanner press does not acquire a page while the moment is waiting for a photograph.</p>

  <p>
    The paper contains only the page a person reads and writes on. The vision call compares the
    returned scan with the original image, so the page needs no machine-readable registration layer.
  </p>

  <PaperTrip
    title="The paper round trip"
    desc="An image model draws the page from screened text. The hub prints it and keeps the original image. A camera or scanner captures the returned sheet. A vision model compares both images and describes added marks, if any. The result informs the continuation."
    caption="The vision model compares the returned sheet with the original image kept for printing. An uncertain reading leaves the activity waiting."
    words={{ title: "Words", sub: ["already through the safety gate"] }}
    drawn={{ title: "One image call", sub: ["the whole A4 sheet"] }}
    printed={{
      title: "Printed",
      sub: ["the hub keeps the original image", "for comparison with the return"],
    }}
    person={{ title: "Somebody writes on it", sub: ["or does not"] }}
    scanned={{ title: "Camera or scanner", sub: ["captures the returned sheet"] }}
    read={{ title: "Two images to a model", sub: ["describes the added marks"] }}
    outcome={{ title: "marks · blank", sub: ["descriptions for continuation", "degraded reading: wait"] }}
    feeds="informs the continuation"
    original="stored original"
  />

  <p>
    The branch depends on whether marks were added; the descriptions inform the continuation. Retained profile observations and the development exception are described under <a href="/en/#memory">system memory</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>The model-call path</h2>

  <p>The generation path selects a method, devises the activity, repairs refused format or structure when permitted, and screens the resulting text with Content Safety. The API then stores the activity for parent approval. A separate model rereads it in a background task after the response. That diagnostic judgement can fail or be skipped at the usage limit without removing the stored activity; it is not a blocking approval gate.</p>

  <figure class="graph">
    <div class="graph-frame" tabindex="0" role="group" aria-labelledby="calls-t">
      <img
        src="/images/calls-en.svg"
        alt="The panel sends method names to the model and receives a form and a move. It then requests the complete activity and receives one JSON document. A format refusal returns the original answer for repair. A structural refusal returns the document with the failed checks for one repair attempt. Finally the document text goes to the safety gate."
        width="833"
        height="721"
      />
    </div>
    <figcaption id="calls-t">
      This diagram covers generation and its conditional repairs, ending with text screening. Its method count and approximate prompt length illustrate earlier research runs, not current service limits. The diagnostic background judge and the calls made while an activity runs are separate.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p>During execution, image generation draws a page, vision reads returned material and a planned <code>ask</code> generates a checked continuation. A scanner comparison also schedules a separate observation for profile calibration; photographic reads skip that observation. These calls are not all repeated at every moment.</p>

  <h2>Objects and portal photographs</h2>
  <p>An object or construction can be returned without a printed page. The vision model receives one photograph and describes the relevant visible material, rather than comparing it with an original. An uncertain reading leaves the activity waiting.</p>
  <p>An invited adolescent can upload a photograph through the portal. The API stores it; the hub retrieves pending photographs on its synchronization cycle, normally every 60 seconds plus network and processing time. A compatible waiting camera moment can receive it through the same reading path. Otherwise the hub archives it or shows it on a display explicitly assigned to photographs. The portal's completed state means the hub finished processing; it does not promise a model response. A disconnected hub leaves it pending.</p>

  <p><a href={NOTEBOOK}>research/officina.ipynb</a> records requests and responses for a local research run. Its saved outputs are an example with synthetic material, not a trace of every production execution.</p>
</Base>
